Kernel density estimation (revised)         [STB-16: snp6]; [STB-X: snp6.x]
-----------------------------------

   ^kerneld1^  varname [^if^ exp] [^in^ range],
		^b^width^(^#^)^ ^k^ercode^(^#^)^ ^np^oint^(^#^)^
		[^g^en^(^densivar gridvar^)^ ^nog^raph graph_options]




Description
-----------

This program calculates the density of ^varname^ using univariate kernel 
estimators as described in Silverman (1986), Fox(1990), Haerdle (1991), and 
Scott (1992). As the default result this command draws the graph of the density
estimations over the grid points used for calculations connected by a line and 
using the symbol ".".

Options
-------

^b^width^(^#^)^ specifies the smoothing parameter (bandwidth or halfwidth) of 
     the kernel. This parameter define the width of the weight function window
     around each grid point. The "smoothness" of the resulting estimate can be
     regulated by changing this parameter: wide intervals produce smooth 
     results; narrow intervals result in noiser density values.

^k^ercode^(^#^)^ specifies the weight function (kernel) to calculate the density
     according to the following numerical codes:

	1 = Uniform
 	2 = Triangle 
 	3 = Epanechnikov
 	4 = Quartic (Biweight)
 	5 = Triweight
 	6 = Gaussian
 	7 = Cosinus

Except for the Gaussian kernel all the functions are supported on [-1,1].

^np^oint^(^#^)^ specifies the number of equally spaced points (grid) in the 
     range of ^varname^ used for the density estimation, as suggested by several
     authors Chambers et. al. 1983; Silverman, 1986; Fox, 1990; Haerdle, 1991;
     Scott, 1992) in order to simplify the number of aritmetical operations.  
     Take into account that, as	the number of points increases, the time for 
     calculation increases too. On the other hand, if the number of points is
     less than 50, the approximation may be too rough to be useful.

^b^width, ^k^ercode, and ^np^point are not optional, the user must input their
     values. If	not, the program halts and displays an error message on screen.

^g^en^(^densivar gridvar^)^ permits to create two variables containing 
     respectively the estimated densities and the corresponding grid points 
     used for calculation.

^nog^raph suppresses the graph drawing

graph_options are any of the options allowed with ^graph, twoway^.


This procedure can be regarded as a descriptive smoother of histograms besides 
a nonparametric density estimator. The density estimator using a triangular
weight function is like a histogram using bins with the specified width but no
fixed origin; the bin's windows run along the series with each observation at
its midpoint allowing overlapping. The other kernels, besides having no fixed
origin use symmetric "smooth" windows.


Example
-------

  . ^kerneld1 snow, bwidth(10) kercode(4) npoint(50)^

Will display the density estimation of ^snow^ using a smoothing parameter of 10,
the Quartic (biweight) kernel and 50 grid points.

  . ^kerneld1 snow, b(10) k(2) np(100) gen(dtri10 gtri10) nog^

Will calculate the density estimate for ^snow^ using a bandwidth of 10, a 
triangular weight function, 100 gridpoints and will create two variables with
the resulting densities and gridpoints without any graphical display.



References
----------

   Chambers, J.M., W.S. Cleveland, B. Kleiner and P.A. Tukey (1983)
        Graphical Methods for Data Analysis. Wadsworth & Brooks/Cole
        Chapter 2: 9-46.
   Fox, J. (1990) Describing univariate distributions. In (Fox, J. &
        J.S. Long, Eds.) "Modern Methods of Data Analysis". Sage
        Chapter 2: 58-125.
   Haerdle, W. (1991) Smoothing Techniques with Implementation in S.
        Springer-Verlag Chapter 2: 43-84.
   Salgado-Ugarte, I.H., M. Shimizu, and T. Taniuchi (1993) snp6: Exploring
        the shape of univariate data using kernel density estimators. Stata 
        Technical Bulletin 16: 8-19.
   Salgado-Ugarte, I.H., M. Shimizu, and T. Taniuchi (1995) snp6.1: ASH,
        WARPing, and kernel density estimation for univariate data. Stata
        Technical Bulletin XX: xx-xx.
   Salgado-Ugarte, I.H., M. Shimizu, and T. Taniuchi (199x) snp6.2: Univariate
        kernel density estimators (Update). Stata Technical Bulletin XX: xx-xx.
   Scott, D.W. (1992) Multivariate Density Estimation: Theory, Practice,
        and Visualization. John Wiley Chapter 6: 125-143.
   Silverman, B.W. (1986) Density Estimation for Statistics and Data 
        Analysis. Chapman and Hall.


